  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nLingering wildfire smoke particles has prompted a Code Orange\nAir Quality Alert (issued by the Pennsylvania Dept. of\nEnvironmental Protection) across portions of western \nPennsylvania today. Smoke will likely exit the region, from \nsouth to north, as a new disturbance tracks into the Great \nLakes and shifts the upper-level wind profile. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA strong low-pressure system will continue to track through the\nGreat Lakes over the next 24 to 48 hours, bringing an increased\nrisk of severe weather to the region on Tuesday. The combination\nof abundant instability (1500J/kg to 2500J/kg of CAPE) fueled by\nwarm, humid air and strong wind shear (35kt to 45kts of\neffective shear) will create a favorable environment for\norganized severe thunderstorms. As a result, the Storm\nPrediction Center continues to hold the Enhanced Risk (Level 3\nout of 5) for severe weather across our entire region. The\nlikelihood of a Severe T-Storm or a Tornado Watch during the\nafternoon hours of Tuesday are considered very high. \n\nResidents should prepare for damaging wind gusts, though \ntornadoes and large hail are also possible with any discrete \nsupercell storms that are able to develop. Severe weather\nanalogs associated with similar synoptic patterns in the summer\nseason strongly support damaging winds over our region to be \nthe primary threat. \n\nShowers and isolated non-severe storms may track through the \nregion early Tuesday morning in the wake of the outflow from a \npassing mesoscale convective system (MCS) to our west. However, \nthe main event for strong to severe storms appears favorable \nbetween 12pm and 8pm Tuesday. \n\nAnomalously high moisture levels with PWATS reaching the 80th \nto 90th percentile raise the risk of flash flooding Tuesday \nevening. Training storms could potentially create localized \nswaths of 3-4 inches of rainfall in a short period of time.\nLocations that saw significant rainfall from this past weekend's\nthunderstorm activity may be especially vulnerable to flash\nflooding due to already saturated soils and increased runoff. \nThe Weather Prediction Center (WPC) has issued a Slight Risk for\nportions of region. High-resolution model guidance continues to\nincrease the probability of rainfall exceeding flash flood\nguidance, with 30-50% probabilities within a 25 mile radius. As\nconfidence grows, a Flood Watch will likely be issued in the\nnext 24 to 36 hours. Current model guidance highlights the\ngreatest potential for excessive rainfall south of Pittsburgh,\nwhere enhanced low-level convergence across eastern Ohio may\npromote back-building thunderstorms. \n\nExpect lingering showers and non-severe thunderstorms into early\nWednesday before a cold front tracks through the region after\nsunrise. \n\nThe potential for precipitation remain lows through at least \nSunday under high pressure.",
